    "result_text": "The full test suite passes. The implementation is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented a daily owner-registration limit that rejects creating an owner once 100 or more owners have already been registered today (by `registrationDate`), responding with **429 Too Many Requests**. I followed the existing guard/exception/advice pattern already established for rules like the per-city capacity limit.\n\nChanges:\n\n1. **New exception** \u2014 `rest/advice/OwnerDailyRegistrationLimitException.java`: a `RuntimeException` carrying the offending date, a client-facing detail message, and `registrationDate` as the error field. Modeled on `OwnerCityAtCapacityException`.\n\n2. **New handler** in `rest/advice/ExceptionControllerAdvice.java`: `@ExceptionHandler(OwnerDailyRegistrationLimitException.class)` returning `HttpStatus.TOO_MANY_REQUESTS` (429) with a `ProblemDetail` body, matching how the other owner-rule handlers render responses.\n\n3. **New guard** in `rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`:\n   - Added `MAX_OWNERS_PER_DAY = 100`.\n   - Added `rejectDailyRegistrationLimitReached()`, which counts existing owners whose `registrationDate` equals `LocalDate.now()` and throws the new exception when that count is \u2265 100.\n   - Wired the call into `addOwner(...)` alongside the other pre-save rejection checks.\n\nThe existing `AcceptanceTest.coreRejectsOverDailyLimit` (creates 100 owners dated today, then expects the 101st to be rejected with 429) passes, and the full `./mvnw test` run is green.",
